/**
@mainpage Rubik's Cube Documentation

@section overview Overview

This project implements a 3x3x3 Rubik's Cube in C++17. The @ref RubiksCube
class is a self-contained model of the puzzle: it stores the 54 stickers,
performs face turns, checks whether the cube is solved, generates random
placements, and reads and writes cube states through files and streams. A
separate console front end, @ref ConsoleUI, exposes the model through a small
interactive menu. The project is built with CMake and is covered by UnitTest++
unit tests.

@section capabilities Required class capabilities

The model fulfils the requirements of the assignment:

- Encapsulation: all 54 stickers are private and are accessed through
  @ref RubiksCube::at, @ref RubiksCube::set, and the public operations.
- Copy construction and copy assignment produce independent deep copies;
  self-assignment is safe.
- A destructor is provided (trivially, because the class owns no dynamic
  resources).
- Comparison with `==` and `!=` compares every sticker of two cubes.
- Stream input with `>>` and stream output with `<<`.
- File input and output through @ref RubiksCube::loadFromFile and
  @ref RubiksCube::saveToFile.
- Random initial placement, face rotation, and a solved-state check.
- The declaration lives in `include/RubiksCube.h`, the implementation in
  `src/RubiksCube.cpp`, and the class has no dependency on the user interface.

@section color_scheme Canonical color scheme

The default constructor creates the canonical solved cube. Every face is
uniform and uses these colors:

| Face  | Outward normal | Solved color |
|-------|----------------|--------------|
| Up    | +y             | White        |
| Down  | -y             | Yellow       |
| Left  | -x             | Orange       |
| Right | +x             | Red          |
| Front | +z             | Green        |
| Back  | -z             | Blue         |

The sticker colors are therefore White, Yellow, Green, Blue, Red, and Orange,
abbreviated `W`, `Y`, `G`, `B`, `R`, and `O`.

@section rotation Convention and coordinate system

The model uses a right-handed coordinate system: +x points right, +y points up,
and +z points toward the viewer (front). The outward normal of each face is the
one listed in the color-scheme table above.

Each face stores nine stickers in row-major order. Row 0 is the top row and
column 0 is the left column as seen from outside that face. When looking
directly at a face, the on-screen right and down directions are:

- Front: (+x, -y)
- Back:  (-x, -y)
- Up:    (+x, +z)
- Down:  (+x, -z)
- Right: (-z, -y)
- Left:  (+z, -y)

A clockwise turn is a -90 degree rotation about the face's outward normal; a
counter-clockwise turn is +90 degrees about the same normal. The whole layer
of 21 stickers (9 on the face and 12 on the adjacent strips) moves as one rigid
body: colors travel with their positions and face orientations travel with
their normals. Every turn is therefore a permutation, and the number of
stickers of each color never changes.

See @ref RubiksCube::rotate for the operation and @ref RubiksCube::Turn for the
two directions.

@section sticker_format Sticker, file, and stream format

A cube state is a sequence of exactly 54 color characters, written as six
lines of nine characters each. The face order is Up, Down, Left, Right, Front,
Back, and the nine characters of a face are its stickers in row-major order,
from the top-left sticker to the bottom-right sticker as seen from outside.
The colors are encoded as `W` (White), `Y` (Yellow), `G` (Green), `B` (Blue),
`R` (Red), and `O` (Orange).

A solved cube is written as:

```
WWWWWWWWW
YYYYYYYYY
OOOOOOOOO
RRRRRRRRR
GGGGGGGGG
BBBBBBBBB
```

On output, `operator<<` writes exactly six lines of nine uppercase characters.
On input, `operator>>` skips leading and embedded whitespace, accepts
uppercase or lowercase color letters, and reads exactly 54 valid characters.
The target cube is modified only when all 54 characters are valid; otherwise
`failbit` is set and the cube is left unchanged.

@section random Random placement

@ref RubiksCube::randomize(std::mt19937&) fills the cube with a random
permutation of the sticker colors, always placing exactly nine stickers of each
color. The caller supplies the generator, so the sequence is reproducible.
The overload @ref RubiksCube::randomize() seeds a `std::mt19937` from
`std::random_device`. The resulting placement need not be solvable.

@section file_io Loading and saving

@ref RubiksCube::loadFromFile reads a state from a text file using the format
above and returns `true` on success. If the file cannot be opened or parsed,
it returns `false` and leaves the cube unchanged. @ref RubiksCube::saveToFile
writes the state in the same format and returns `true` on success, or `false`
if the file cannot be opened or written.

@section solved Solved check

@ref RubiksCube::isSolved returns `true` only when every face is uniform and
each face has its canonical solved color. A cube with uniform faces but a
different color arrangement is not solved.

@section separation Separation of model and console UI

@ref RubiksCube is a pure model. It does not print prompts or read commands,
and it can be embedded in any front end. @ref ConsoleUI owns a cube, uses only
the public model API, and communicates through the input and output streams
passed to its constructor. This keeps the puzzle logic independent of the menu
text and of `std::cin`/`std::cout`, and makes the model directly testable.

@section build_run Building, running, testing, and documentation

Build the project from its root directory:

```
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build
```

Run the console application:

```
./build/rubiks_cube_app
```

The menu offers showing the cube, randomizing it, loading a state from a file,
rotating a face, checking whether it is solved, saving a state to a file, and
exiting. Faces are entered as `U`, `D`, `L`, `R`, `F`, or `B`; directions are
entered as `c` for clockwise or `a` for counter-clockwise.

Run the unit tests:

```
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
```

The UnitTest++ suite covers at least 30 test cases. UnitTest++ is fetched
automatically by CMake when it is not installed.

Generate this documentation with Doxygen from the project root:

```
doxygen Doxyfile
```

The HTML output is written to `docs/generated/html/index.html`. The
`docs/generated/` directory is ignored by Git, as is the `build/` directory.

@section layout Project layout

- `include/RubiksCube.h` — model declaration and API documentation
- `include/ConsoleUI.h` — console front-end declaration
- `src/RubiksCube.cpp` — cube storage, turns, I/O, and solved check
- `src/ConsoleUI.cpp` — interactive menu implementation
- `src/main.cpp` — program entry point
- `tests/test_main.cpp`, `tests/test_cube.cpp` — UnitTest++ suite
- `data/` — example solved and scrambled cube states
- `docs/mainpage.dox` — this documentation main page
- `Doxyfile` — Doxygen configuration
- `README.md` — build, run, and documentation quick start
- `CMakeLists.txt` — CMake build and test configuration
*/
